  "requirement": "Before drafting, the `habeas-requirements` skill MUST direct agents to ask the user whether uncommitted edits to the book that the agent did not make in the current task belong to the task.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Obligation.** The requirement has one clause. Before drafting, the `habeas-requirements` skill MUST direct agents to ask the user whether uncommitted book edits they did not make in the current task belong to the task. That gives two testable parts:\n- The skill contains the instruction to ask.\n- The instruction comes before the drafting step.\n\n**Test.** `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_requirements_skill_asks_about_foreign_edits_before_drafting]` is marked `{test}` for the requirement. It does three things:\n- It calls `step_of` to find the workflow step of the `habeas-requirements` skill that contains \"If it has uncommitted edits you did not make in this task, ask the user whether they belong to it\". `step_of` panics if no step contains the phrase. That covers both the condition (uncommitted edits the agent did not make) and the action (ask the user whether they belong to the task).\n- It asserts that the same step contains \"Run `git status` on the book's source directory\". That ties the instruction to checking the book's source directory.\n- It asserts that the ask step's number is lower than the number of the step containing \"Write drafts\". That covers \"before drafting\".\n\n**Helpers.** `workflow_steps` parses the numbered `- [ ] N.` steps of the skill's Workflow section. It joins the indented continuation lines and collapses the text. A phrase wrapped across source lines therefore still matches. `step` and `step_of` build on it.\n\n**Skill text.** In `assets/skills/habeas-requirements/SKILL.md`, step 1 of the Workflow section holds the ask instruction and the `git status` check. Step 2 (\"Draft. Write drafts ...\") is the only step with \"Write drafts\". The test's strings match the real text, and the ordering assertion compares step 1 with step 2.\n\n**Gaps.** I found none. The requirement is documentation-style text, and it names no input classes or failure modes beyond presence and ordering. The test asserts both. It has no negative case, such as an unrelated step that mentions the phrase. The requirement does not call for one.",
